✅ HashSet vs TreeSet

| Feature                  | **HashSet**                                   | **TreeSet**                                              |
| ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Ordering**             | ❌ No guaranteed order                         | ✅ Sorted (natural order or `Comparator`)                 |
| **Performance**          | ✅ Average `O(1)` add/contains/remove          | `O(log n)` add/contains/remove                           |
| **Underlying structure** | **HashMap** internally                        | **TreeMap** / Red-Black Tree internally                  |
| **Null elements**        | ✅ One null                                    | ❌ Null → `NullPointerException` (natural ordering)       |
| **Element requirement**  | Good `equals`/`hashCode`                      | Must be `Comparable` **or** provide `Comparator`         |
| **Implements**           | `Set`                                         | `NavigableSet` → `SortedSet` → `Set`                     |
| **Thread-safe?**         | ❌ No                                          | ❌ No                                                     |
| **Use case**             | Fast unique collection                        | Sorted unique collection / range ops                     |

────────────────────────────────────────
🧠 Examples

```java
// HashSet — unordered
Set<String> hashSet = new HashSet<>();
hashSet.add("Banana");
hashSet.add("Apple");
hashSet.add("Orange");
System.out.println(hashSet); // order unpredictable

// TreeSet — sorted by natural order
Set<String> treeSet = new TreeSet<>();
treeSet.add("Banana");
treeSet.add("Apple");
treeSet.add("Orange");
System.out.println(treeSet); // [Apple, Banana, Orange]

// Navigable helpers (TreeSet implements NavigableSet)
// Sorted set: [Apple, Banana, Orange]
TreeSet<String> nav = new TreeSet<>(treeSet);
nav.lower("Banana");   // greatest element < "Banana"  → "Apple"
nav.higher("Banana");  // least element > "Banana"     → "Orange"
nav.floor("Banana");   // greatest element <= "Banana" → "Banana"
nav.ceiling("Banana"); // least element >= "Banana"    → "Banana"
```

Why this matters:
HashSet cannot do “give me the next/previous sorted value” efficiently.
TreeSet can, because elements are kept sorted in a tree.

Custom comparator:

```java
Set<String> byLength = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparingInt(String::length));
```

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Summary
- Don’t care about order + want speed → **HashSet**
- Need **sorted** unique elements / range queries → **TreeSet**
- Need **insertion order** → **LinkedHashSet** (not TreeSet)

| Need                 | Use              |
| -------------------- | ---------------- |
| Fast, unordered set  | `HashSet`        |
| Insertion-order set  | `LinkedHashSet`  |
| Sorted set           | `TreeSet`        |

🎤 Quick interview traps
1) TreeSet sorts elements; LinkedHashSet keeps insertion order — different goals.
2) `treeSet.add(null)` → NPE with natural ordering.
3) Elements must be mutually comparable (or supply Comparator) — otherwise `ClassCastException`.
4) HashSet uniqueness uses `equals`/`hashCode`; TreeSet uniqueness uses compare (`compareTo` / Comparator) — inconsistent compare/equals can surprise you.
5) Both are not thread-safe.
